WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.434 --> 00:00:07.067
-Last "Corrections" of 2021.
Welcome, everybody.

00:00:07.067 --> 00:00:09.200
So excited to jump
right into it.

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:13.767
I referred to Philadelphia
Cream Cheese as "Philly's Best."

00:00:13.767 --> 00:00:17.033
Philly's Best
is a cheesesteak restaurant,

00:00:17.033 --> 00:00:18.734
and I realized my error

00:00:18.734 --> 00:00:22.267
when someone from Philadelphia
threw a battery at me.

00:00:25.033 --> 00:00:28.033
Also known as a
Philadelphia How Do You Do?

00:00:30.701 --> 00:00:35.467
We talked about the anime
"Sailor Moon"

00:00:35.467 --> 00:00:37.334
as if it was a current show.

00:00:37.334 --> 00:00:41.734
We were aware that we were
probably wrong about that,

00:00:41.734 --> 00:00:43.434
and someone did write and say

00:00:43.434 --> 00:00:45.901
the final episode
of "Sailor Moon"

00:00:45.901 --> 00:00:50.234
was February 8th, 1997.

00:00:50.234 --> 00:00:51.834
And I would just say to
that person, the next time

00:00:51.834 --> 00:00:56.734
you give that information
to someone, just the year.

00:00:56.734 --> 00:00:58.868
You know, based on
their reaction,

00:00:58.868 --> 00:01:04.400
I think that'll let you know
if they want the month and day.

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:09.734
Oh, so this was interesting.

00:01:09.734 --> 00:01:11.300
So we did a "Day Drinking"
with Will Forte.

00:01:11.300 --> 00:01:13.534
I highly recommend you watch it.
It was great.

00:01:13.534 --> 00:01:16.901
And we showed Edvard Munch
"The Scream,"

00:01:16.901 --> 00:01:20.634
and Will was doing voices
of the paintings

00:01:20.634 --> 00:01:24.067
and he, as you would expect,
screamed, and someone said,

00:01:24.067 --> 00:01:26.667
"You know, the person in this
painting is not screaming.

00:01:26.667 --> 00:01:30.434
They're reacting to a scream,"

00:01:30.434 --> 00:01:32.000
which I didn't know,

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:35.000
and I guess Edvard Munch
only saw --

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:37.200
I mean, imagine
if you were there

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:38.801
and you missed the screen.

00:01:41.067 --> 00:01:42.968
You know, you hear a scream
and then you run over

00:01:42.968 --> 00:01:44.734
and you're like,
"Could you do it again?

00:01:44.734 --> 00:01:46.834
I'd love to paint it."

00:01:46.834 --> 00:01:49.267
"No, no.

00:01:49.267 --> 00:01:51.133
I'm not going to do it again."

00:01:51.133 --> 00:01:52.234
"I was like, yeah,
but I'm just --

00:01:52.234 --> 00:01:53.467
I'm a painter and I feel like

00:01:53.467 --> 00:01:57.667
that's what people
are going to want to see."

00:01:57.667 --> 00:02:02.834
"You could paint
someone reacting to it."

00:02:02.834 --> 00:02:04.701
But this is also fascinating,

00:02:04.701 --> 00:02:07.834
because we also showed
the "Mona Lisa."

00:02:07.834 --> 00:02:10.100
And I was told this
is actually a woman

00:02:10.100 --> 00:02:12.968
looking at a woman
named Mona Lisa.

00:02:14.734 --> 00:02:17.300
[ Laughter ]

00:02:17.300 --> 00:02:20.434
And according to Michelangelo,

00:02:20.434 --> 00:02:23.167
what he wanted to do
was paint a woman

00:02:23.167 --> 00:02:26.000
who was friends with
a woman named Mona Lisa,

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:29.667
and Mona Lisa's always late.

00:02:29.667 --> 00:02:31.634
And so this woman
is always waiting for her.

00:02:31.634 --> 00:02:34.634
And then, one day --

00:02:34.634 --> 00:02:36.367
And you know, when you have
a friend who's always late

00:02:36.367 --> 00:02:37.534
and you're just waiting,

00:02:37.534 --> 00:02:39.767
and you know they're gonna be
full of excuses.

00:02:39.767 --> 00:02:43.033
And so Mona Lisa
basically sees her

00:02:43.033 --> 00:02:45.167
and does that like --
you know, the like --

00:02:47.234 --> 00:02:49.367
Does that, and then that
thing where you like,

00:02:49.367 --> 00:02:50.834
speed up, jog a little bit,

00:02:50.834 --> 00:02:53.033
like, "Oh, I'm going to
try to close the gap

00:02:53.033 --> 00:02:54.200
of how late I am,"

00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:58.901
and then slipped
and just ate it.

00:02:58.901 --> 00:03:01.167
So that's the little smile.

00:03:01.167 --> 00:03:03.934
It's like if
your friend was super late

00:03:03.934 --> 00:03:06.234
and then, like,
fell really hard.

00:03:06.234 --> 00:03:08.801
This woman's name is
Debbie Schwartz.

00:03:08.801 --> 00:03:11.100
[ Laughter ]

00:03:15.067 --> 00:03:17.834
I often say,
when I learn something, I say,

00:03:17.834 --> 00:03:20.200
"Oh, this is interesting,"
and someone told me,

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:23.367
"You shouldn't say that,
because it's not conclusive.

00:03:23.367 --> 00:03:26.434
You should say,
'I find this interesting.'"

00:03:26.434 --> 00:03:29.400
But problem with that is,
I don't.

00:03:31.667 --> 00:03:34.634
[ Laughter ]

00:03:34.634 --> 00:03:36.200
I mispronounced
"target audience"

00:03:36.200 --> 00:03:39.267
and said "target ardience."

00:03:39.267 --> 00:03:41.300
But that is on purpose.

00:03:41.300 --> 00:03:42.934
Carol Burnett would
always do this to

00:03:42.934 --> 00:03:46.567
signal to her grandmother.

00:03:46.567 --> 00:03:48.167
So every now and then,
I will say a word

00:03:48.167 --> 00:03:51.367
pirate-style
for the Sea Captain.

00:03:51.367 --> 00:03:55.534
Now I will say,
with "target audience,"

00:03:55.534 --> 00:03:57.501
I just should have said
"tarrrget audience."

00:03:57.501 --> 00:03:59.100
I feel like to go
"target arrdience"

00:03:59.100 --> 00:04:01.067
was a bit of --
it was a stretch.

00:04:05.501 --> 00:04:09.234
What did I get wrong here?
Okay. I said --

00:04:09.234 --> 00:04:15.033
We made a joke about Don Jr.

00:04:15.033 --> 00:04:18.033
being in
"The Fellowship of the Rings."

00:04:18.033 --> 00:04:19.300
My bad.

00:04:19.300 --> 00:04:24.834
It's "Lord of the Rings:
Fellowship of the Ring,"

00:04:24.834 --> 00:04:26.000
which I can't believe I forgot,

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:31.868
because it just
rolls off the tongue.

00:04:31.868 --> 00:04:33.167
We're going to start
calling this

00:04:33.167 --> 00:04:36.534
"Late Night with Seth Meyers:
A Late Night Show."

00:04:36.534 --> 00:04:38.868
[ Laughter ]

00:04:41.300 --> 00:04:43.701
I talked about
my beautiful baby girl,

00:04:43.701 --> 00:04:47.234
and I have forgotten to mention
that her middle name is Ruth,

00:04:47.234 --> 00:04:52.300
after my wife's grandmother,
and her first name is Adelaide.

00:04:52.300 --> 00:04:55.267
And like four of you,

00:04:55.267 --> 00:04:58.734
after I said her name
was Adelaide Ruth Meyers, said,

00:04:58.734 --> 00:05:02.367
"Oh, so her initials are ARM?"

00:05:02.367 --> 00:05:04.567
Like it was a burn.

00:05:05.133 --> 00:05:07.133
[ Laughter ]

00:05:07.133 --> 00:05:09.467
Like she's going
to be in school one day

00:05:09.467 --> 00:05:12.701
and a mean kid's going to go,
"What's up, ARM?"

00:05:12.701 --> 00:05:14.968
"What?

00:05:14.968 --> 00:05:16.033
Sorry, what did you say?"

00:05:16.033 --> 00:05:19.400
"ARM.
That's your name, isn't it?

00:05:19.400 --> 00:05:22.367
Addie Ruth Meyers, ARM?"

00:05:22.367 --> 00:05:24.367
"Uh, yeah, I guess.

00:05:24.367 --> 00:05:26.067
I'm sorry,
what's your point?"

00:05:26.067 --> 00:05:27.601
"I thought you'd be
with Leslie...

00:05:27.601 --> 00:05:29.868
Garrison."

00:05:29.868 --> 00:05:31.167
"What are you talking about?"

00:05:31.167 --> 00:05:34.801
"Leslie Erica Garrison. LEG?

00:05:34.801 --> 00:05:36.734
I thought you guys
would be hanging out.

00:05:38.868 --> 00:05:40.467
Bye."

00:05:43.934 --> 00:05:49.734
We made a Clippy joke and said
this was a joke from 1995.

00:05:49.734 --> 00:05:54.167
Clippy was actually
introduced in 1997.

00:05:54.167 --> 00:05:58.334
To soften the blow after
"Sailor Moon" had been canceled.

00:06:05.601 --> 00:06:07.701
We mentioned "House,"
and you know, again --

00:06:07.701 --> 00:06:09.467
we know at this point,
people who watch the show

00:06:09.467 --> 00:06:12.434
we're basically watching it
for the impressions.

00:06:12.434 --> 00:06:14.100
And we mentioned
Hugh Laurie's House,

00:06:14.100 --> 00:06:15.267
and people were so bummed
I didn't do it.

00:06:15.267 --> 00:06:17.267
It's tricky.

00:06:17.267 --> 00:06:19.167
It's tricky to do
British people who do --

00:06:19.167 --> 00:06:22.334
because I can do Hugh Laurie,
but then you've got to do...

00:06:22.334 --> 00:06:24.000
You can't just do the voice
he does as House.

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:25.701
You've got to go in his mind
as a British person

00:06:25.701 --> 00:06:26.767
and switch it over.

00:06:26.767 --> 00:06:28.133
[ As House ]
I think it's this.

00:06:31.968 --> 00:06:35.267
So, Hugh Grant,
because Hugh Grant is like --

00:06:35.267 --> 00:06:37.834
[ Mumbling as Hugh Grant ]

00:06:37.834 --> 00:06:39.834
It's exhausting,

00:06:39.834 --> 00:06:42.501
but this would be
Hugh Grant as an American.

00:06:42.501 --> 00:06:43.434
Alright, so remember,
here's British.

00:06:43.434 --> 00:06:48.033
It's like -- [ Mumbling ]

00:06:48.033 --> 00:06:49.634
[ In American accent ]
"Welcome to Late Night."

00:06:51.601 --> 00:06:54.534
[ Laughter ]

00:06:54.534 --> 00:06:56.634
I'm so bummed, because --

00:06:56.634 --> 00:06:58.367
Alright, so Matthew McConaughey
was on the show,

00:06:58.367 --> 00:07:00.734
and I'm just bummed because,
you know,

00:07:00.734 --> 00:07:05.033
it's incumbent upon me
as a talk show host

00:07:05.033 --> 00:07:08.767
to know I have a funny thing
to tell a guest,

00:07:08.767 --> 00:07:11.667
and the funny thing I would have
told Matthew McConaughey

00:07:11.667 --> 00:07:13.868
is the other day, my son Axel,

00:07:13.868 --> 00:07:16.400
we're getting him dressed
for school,

00:07:16.400 --> 00:07:18.834
and all of a sudden
I look at him and I realize,

00:07:18.834 --> 00:07:22.267
"Oh, I know exactly
who you look like.

00:07:22.267 --> 00:07:26.033
You look like a character
in a movie,

00:07:26.033 --> 00:07:29.400
and that movie
is 'Dazed and Confused,'

00:07:29.400 --> 00:07:33.100
and Axel,
you look like Wooderson."

00:07:33.968 --> 00:07:36.801
[ Laughter ]

00:07:39.000 --> 00:07:40.934
And if Peter Billingsley
is ever on the show,

00:07:40.934 --> 00:07:42.767
I'm going to tell him
my son looks like

00:07:42.767 --> 00:07:44.968
Ralphie from
"A Christmas Story."

00:07:51.033 --> 00:07:55.234
We kind of talked about
how this might be like a

00:07:55.234 --> 00:07:57.567
"hey, it's the end of the year"
"Corrections" spectacular,

00:07:57.567 --> 00:08:01.801
but I got to be honest,
it was it was too much work.

00:08:04.501 --> 00:08:07.200
I do this alone and I have
too much going on.

00:08:07.200 --> 00:08:08.868
It was too much work.

00:08:08.868 --> 00:08:10.901
I also came up
with the idea too late.

00:08:10.901 --> 00:08:12.968
But now the reality is,
I think March 1st

00:08:12.968 --> 00:08:14.567
is the year anniversary
of "Corrections,"

00:08:14.567 --> 00:08:16.634
so basically, this is
a pre-spectacular.

00:08:16.634 --> 00:08:17.834
We're just going
to move the date,

00:08:17.834 --> 00:08:20.834
and I think now
we'll get a team, obviously.

00:08:20.834 --> 00:08:24.567
I think NBC will
obviously pay for a team.

00:08:24.567 --> 00:08:25.901
But it was fun
and I want to thank,

00:08:25.901 --> 00:08:27.901
because I asked you to send in
your favorite things

00:08:27.901 --> 00:08:31.200
that were #BestOf,
and these are my favorite.

00:08:31.200 --> 00:08:35.567
Someone said, "My favorite
correction was October 25th,

00:08:35.567 --> 00:08:36.634
when Seth said --"

00:08:36.634 --> 00:08:37.667
Alright, so here's what I said.

00:08:37.667 --> 00:08:39.968
I made a mistake
doing a mimed phone call,

00:08:39.968 --> 00:08:41.234
and I said
that really bums me out,

00:08:41.234 --> 00:08:43.767
because I feel like as far
as phone call comedy,

00:08:43.767 --> 00:08:49.367
one-sided phone call comedy,
I am the new Bob Newhart,

00:08:49.367 --> 00:08:51.834
the Bob New-hart,
if you will.

00:08:51.834 --> 00:08:54.434
And this person's favorite
moment was when I made that joke

00:08:54.434 --> 00:09:01.534
and a guy, audibly irritated,
said, "Oh, my God."

00:09:01.534 --> 00:09:03.300
Let's see if you can hear it.

00:09:03.300 --> 00:09:07.400
When it comes to doing
one-sided phone call comedy,

00:09:07.400 --> 00:09:10.434
I am the new Bob Newhart,

00:09:10.434 --> 00:09:13.601
The Bob New-hart, if you will.

00:09:13.601 --> 00:09:15.634
-Oh, my God.

00:09:15.634 --> 00:09:17.267
[ Laughter ]

00:09:17.267 --> 00:09:20.767
-Baze.

00:09:20.767 --> 00:09:23.801
[ Laughter ]

00:09:25.100 --> 00:09:27.300
Let's isolate it.

00:09:27.300 --> 00:09:30.467
-Oh, my God.

00:09:30.467 --> 00:09:32.067
-That you, Baze?

00:09:34.467 --> 00:09:36.067
I need your support.

00:09:39.267 --> 00:09:42.501
Because Baze comes up with
terrible jokes all the time.

00:09:42.501 --> 00:09:45.868
[ Laughter ]

00:09:45.868 --> 00:09:47.734
All the time,
and I don't tell him,

00:09:47.734 --> 00:09:48.901
to save him the embarrassment.

00:09:48.901 --> 00:09:50.801
Like, for example,
another correction I got

00:09:50.801 --> 00:09:53.067
is instead of
"consolation prize,"

00:09:53.067 --> 00:09:55.133
I accidentally said
"constellation prize,"

00:09:55.133 --> 00:09:57.434
and Baze said,
"You should go out and say,

00:09:57.434 --> 00:09:59.601
'It's okay when you say
consolation prize,

00:09:59.601 --> 00:10:01.167
because you're a star.'"

00:10:01.167 --> 00:10:03.434
[ Laughter ]

00:10:08.934 --> 00:10:10.133
Some of the things
people liked, and again,

00:10:10.133 --> 00:10:12.067
when we get to the Spectacular,
March 1st,

00:10:12.067 --> 00:10:14.234
obviously,
we'll tick all these boxes.

00:10:14.234 --> 00:10:16.767
Everybody like the Pixar movie
that we talked about.

00:10:16.767 --> 00:10:18.033
If you guys don't remember,

00:10:18.033 --> 00:10:20.801
the Pixar movie
is about chickens

00:10:20.801 --> 00:10:22.501
that go undercover in Germany
during World War II

00:10:22.501 --> 00:10:26.000
as eggs and then hatched
to help other farm animals,

00:10:26.000 --> 00:10:29.167
resistance farm animals, escape.

00:10:29.167 --> 00:10:33.667
Pixar, very excited, they
gave us a new title for it --

00:10:33.667 --> 00:10:35.234
"Ca-Ca-Call of Duty."

00:10:38.868 --> 00:10:40.133
People love
the graphics department.

00:10:40.133 --> 00:10:42.133
They love that we have
a graphics team

00:10:42.133 --> 00:10:44.567
that makes mistakes and loves

00:10:44.567 --> 00:10:46.067
when they get punished
for those mistakes,

00:10:46.067 --> 00:10:48.767
because they all have
a humiliation fetish.

00:10:48.767 --> 00:10:50.534
And I actually went down
and I was like,

00:10:50.534 --> 00:10:53.434
"Well, congratulations, you
made Best of Corrections,

00:10:53.434 --> 00:10:55.067
and they were so bummed.

00:10:55.067 --> 00:10:56.734
I was like, "What's wrong?"

00:10:56.734 --> 00:11:00.501
They were like, "Well,
isn't there a Worst Of?"

00:11:00.501 --> 00:11:01.968
I was like,
"Fine, you're Worst Of."

00:11:01.968 --> 00:11:04.000
And they were like, "Oh, no."

00:11:06.133 --> 00:11:07.734
And the graphics department,

00:11:07.734 --> 00:11:11.033
every single week,
we do a different mug

00:11:11.033 --> 00:11:12.300
and they find out
in the morning.

00:11:12.300 --> 00:11:15.033
I'm like, "Hey, can you
make something for me?"

00:11:15.033 --> 00:11:16.734
And there's this one.

00:11:16.734 --> 00:11:20.501
I do think that
we've joked about the fact

00:11:20.501 --> 00:11:22.701
that there's
no "Correction" merch.

00:11:22.701 --> 00:11:27.901
I feel like this -- I feel like
that's the mug, right?

00:11:27.901 --> 00:11:32.434
So I set up a meeting with NBC
to go in and basically,

00:11:32.434 --> 00:11:33.567
you know,
raise some hell and be like,

00:11:33.567 --> 00:11:38.234
"Hey, it's time
for 'Corrections' merch."

00:11:38.234 --> 00:11:40.033
At which point, I'm assuming
they're going to do that thing

00:11:40.033 --> 00:11:41.434
where they're like,
"Yeah, absolutely,"

00:11:41.434 --> 00:11:44.434
to cover for the fact they don't
know what I'm talking about.

00:11:44.434 --> 00:11:46.901
"Yeah, absolutely. Yes."

00:11:46.901 --> 00:11:48.501
But the important thing is,
when we sell these,

00:11:48.501 --> 00:11:51.033
every dollar we raise,
which I think is really cool,

00:11:51.033 --> 00:11:52.434
is going to go to me.

00:11:53.300 --> 00:11:54.901
[ Laughter ]

00:12:01.367 --> 00:12:04.734
I think we maybe
will have guests

00:12:04.734 --> 00:12:06.400
when we do the Spectacular.

00:12:06.400 --> 00:12:09.033
Get Patty Lyons here,
our knitting friend,

00:12:09.033 --> 00:12:12.467
Doof Warrior.

00:12:12.467 --> 00:12:13.634
Werner Herzog, maybe.

00:12:13.634 --> 00:12:15.167
I mean, they could send
in video messages,

00:12:15.167 --> 00:12:17.667
Maybe we'll find
a [bleep] sociopath

00:12:17.667 --> 00:12:19.167
that says Lego Bricks.

00:12:23.000 --> 00:12:24.501
We talked about this before
and people complained,

00:12:24.501 --> 00:12:27.200
"Hey, if it's not on TV,
why are there bleeps

00:12:27.200 --> 00:12:28.300
on the YouTube video?"

00:12:28.300 --> 00:12:29.567
YouTube does that.

00:12:29.567 --> 00:12:31.200
YouTube bleeps it.
That's not our fault.

00:12:31.200 --> 00:12:32.634
More troubling to me,

00:12:32.634 --> 00:12:34.834
Someone pointed out,
"You know, sometimes YouTube,

00:12:34.834 --> 00:12:36.133
the closed captioning,"

00:12:36.133 --> 00:12:38.067
which is very valuable
and it means the world to us

00:12:38.067 --> 00:12:39.434
that they closed caption it --

00:12:39.434 --> 00:12:42.133
But sometimes
they put (Laughter).

00:12:42.133 --> 00:12:44.868
Sometimes they put
(Light laughter)."

00:12:44.868 --> 00:12:47.067
[ Laughter ]

00:12:49.667 --> 00:12:52.400
That's not for you to decide.

00:12:57.801 --> 00:13:00.133
"Cicada, Cicada."
I mean, that's really the goal.

00:13:00.133 --> 00:13:03.634
I think March 1st might be
too soon for "Cicada, Cicada."

00:13:03.634 --> 00:13:05.200
Got to write some songs
for "Cicada, Cicada."

00:13:05.200 --> 00:13:07.100
I mean, with the cast
locked down.

00:13:07.100 --> 00:13:10.200
They've all committed
for the next year.

00:13:10.200 --> 00:13:13.000
But, you know, I want to
write a song about,

00:13:13.000 --> 00:13:15.334
I think a beautiful song
would be the cicada finding out

00:13:15.334 --> 00:13:17.300
that they only have, like,
four weeks to live.

00:13:17.300 --> 00:13:20.434
Like, I think a beautiful song
for Ms. Christine Baranski

00:13:20.434 --> 00:13:23.300
would be like, how four weeks
in New York City

00:13:23.300 --> 00:13:24.534
is a lifetime anywhere else.

00:13:24.534 --> 00:13:25.767
I think that's really beautiful.

00:13:25.767 --> 00:13:29.467
And then I have a scene
where the cockroach --

00:13:29.467 --> 00:13:31.200
and this is gonna be
a very topical show.

00:13:31.200 --> 00:13:34.300
It's going to be very tied into
the world we're living in now.

00:13:34.300 --> 00:13:36.734
You know, the Mets-loving
cockroach will come in

00:13:36.734 --> 00:13:38.434
and there will be a scene
that starts

00:13:38.434 --> 00:13:39.667
where he's like, "Bad news.

00:13:39.667 --> 00:13:42.567
I just came back
from the doctor and...

00:13:42.567 --> 00:13:44.367
and I tested positive."

00:13:44.367 --> 00:13:45.767
And they'll say,
"Oh, no. For coronavirus?"

00:13:45.767 --> 00:13:46.767
And he'll say, "No.

00:13:46.767 --> 00:13:48.400
For the Mets!

00:13:50.634 --> 00:13:54.167
♪ The doc says it's as bad
as it gets ♪

00:13:54.167 --> 00:13:56.934
♪ Fever, bad cough,
and the sweats ♪

00:13:59.033 --> 00:14:00.534
♪ I said, Is there a cure? ♪

00:14:00.534 --> 00:14:02.133
♪ He said, "Sure. ♪

00:14:02.133 --> 00:14:06.534
♪ I take a hammer
and I hit you on the head ♪

00:14:06.534 --> 00:14:08.934
♪ Until you forgets ♪

00:14:13.133 --> 00:14:17.334
I'd like to let you guys
behind the curtain

00:14:17.334 --> 00:14:20.734
on "Corrections" a little bit,
which is --

00:14:20.734 --> 00:14:23.334
We now have three kids, and
the 3-month-old has a cough,

00:14:23.334 --> 00:14:24.400
and it's just terrible.

00:14:24.400 --> 00:14:26.567
Kids getting sick is awful.

00:14:26.567 --> 00:14:28.801
And especially, you know,
in this time we're living in.

00:14:28.801 --> 00:14:31.834
everything, you just
worry about what it is.

00:14:31.834 --> 00:14:34.334
And you know, for my wife,

00:14:34.334 --> 00:14:37.534
she's got this 3-month-old
that has this cough.

00:14:37.534 --> 00:14:39.901
And you know,
our boys, they're 3 and 5,

00:14:39.901 --> 00:14:43.400
and no matter
how much you try to tell them,

00:14:43.400 --> 00:14:44.868
like, it's
a really stressful morning,

00:14:44.868 --> 00:14:46.300
they don't understand.

00:14:46.300 --> 00:14:47.634
Like, they're loving
and they're sweet,

00:14:47.634 --> 00:14:48.634
but they don't understand,
and they're just gonna

00:14:48.634 --> 00:14:49.968
run around and be themselves.

00:14:49.968 --> 00:14:53.667
So the only person in my
wife's life who lives with her,

00:14:53.667 --> 00:14:56.701
who could understand and
behave differently is me.

00:14:56.701 --> 00:14:58.367
And yet this morning,
while this was all happening,

00:14:58.367 --> 00:15:01.167
I was...

00:15:01.167 --> 00:15:04.767
writing down lyrics of things
that rhymed with the Mets.

00:15:04.767 --> 00:15:05.801
[ Laughter ]

00:15:05.801 --> 00:15:07.734
And like,
singing to myself, like --

00:15:13.701 --> 00:15:15.667
♪ Bernie Goetz ♪

00:15:18.467 --> 00:15:19.968
I feel terrible, you know.

00:15:19.968 --> 00:15:21.000
But then she comes over
and she's like,

00:15:21.000 --> 00:15:22.834
"What are you doing?"

00:15:22.834 --> 00:15:24.434
And I was like,
"Well, I'm writing lyrics

00:15:24.434 --> 00:15:26.033
for a Broadway musical."

00:15:29.167 --> 00:15:31.734
And she was like,
"You can't do this right now."

00:15:31.734 --> 00:15:32.868
And I was like, "Do you think

00:15:32.868 --> 00:15:34.868
Sondheim's wife
ever said that to him?"

00:15:34.868 --> 00:15:36.501
And she was like,
"Sondheim never had a wife."

00:15:36.501 --> 00:15:38.167
And I said, "Exactly!"

00:15:39.868 --> 00:15:41.501
[ Laughter ]

00:15:44.734 --> 00:15:50.601
Alright, so like I said,
I blew it

00:15:50.601 --> 00:15:58.200
in that I promised a Spectacular
that didn't happen.

00:15:58.200 --> 00:16:01.534
I do want to give you
a treat, though.

00:16:01.534 --> 00:16:04.100
One thing that people always ask
about with this show is,

00:16:04.100 --> 00:16:06.767
"Hey, how come we never see
Mikey the Shoe?"

00:16:06.767 --> 00:16:09.400
People want to see him,
and he doesn't want to be seen,

00:16:09.400 --> 00:16:14.767
but it's, I don't know,
maybe consider a Christmas gift.

00:16:14.767 --> 00:16:17.868
So anyway, for everybody at home
who's never seen him,

00:16:17.868 --> 00:16:20.701
do please enjoy Mikey the Shoe.

00:16:20.701 --> 00:16:24.434
-Ohhh, you don't never put
Mikey the Shoe's face on camera!

00:16:27.267 --> 00:16:28.634
-Why?

00:16:28.634 --> 00:16:33.400
-'Cause I got people
who are lookin' for me!

00:16:33.467 --> 00:16:35.934
[ Laughter ]

00:16:42.501 --> 00:16:46.968
-And I'm sorry, we have to pay
you guys overtime for that?

00:16:50.400 --> 00:16:52.667
I'm lucky I've got
that mug money about to roll in.

00:16:54.701 --> 00:16:56.968
See me in two weeks.
Happy holidays, everybody.

